[Music]  we're going to let you in on a little  secret  one that's maybe not actually that much  of a secret sometimes  we don't know what we're looking at  we'll find a microbe  that looks familiar but the name of it  eludes us  fortunately we don't need to know the  name of something to show it off  so when these situations arrive we'll go  with a name  that is as specific as we can get like  the ciliate here  that we refer to so specifically as  ciliate but with our new differential  interference  contrast microscope we've been able to  make out all sorts of new details  that we couldn't see before and in some  cases  those new details have helped us  identify  organisms that we could not name before  like if you look closer here you might  be able to make out this little bit of a  crystalline organelle that little tiny  thing is called the organelle of libra  [ __ ]  though it's also known as the watch  glass organelle because  it resembles a watch glass what does it  do  we're not completely sure though it  might be something related to  phototaxis which is how you move toward  or away from  light but what it helps us do is  identify what we are looking at and give  this ciliate the more specific name  of ophiroglina ophiroglina  is a genus of histophagus ciliates  meaning that they feed on  tissue the one we're watching here has  been drawn to this  crustacean like a shark smelling a drop  of blood and water  except it's a microbe drawn to a crack  in its shell  and once it gets inside the ophiroglina  gorges on the tissue inside  so the ophiroglinom seems like it should  be easy enough to find  we just need to keep an eye out for the  watch glass organelle  and other dead organisms but one of the  reasons  it's hard to identify these organisms is  because they do not  always look like this some ciliates have  life cycles  that take them through different shapes  and sizes  and activities and responses it may be  the same organism  but the way that it looks or behaves  changes  ophiroglina's life cycle is made up of  five  established stages as well as a few  additional though more rare  possibilities  the organism starts in its theran stage  taking on an elongated shape as it swims  freely about the microcosmos  it's moving a bit quickly so it might  not be very apparent but at this point  the watch glass organelle  should be visible the third stage  doesn't have food vacuoles yet but it  still seeks  food it'll swim around for about two  days  chasing chemical stimuli and light that  pointed in the general direction of food  but once they settle down into a more  restful state  they begin to fill up with food vacuoles  and  move on to their next stage the  ophiroglina becomes  more opaque as it transitions into its  equivalent of adulthood  the trophon stage at this point the  swimming trophant will still be drawn to  light  perhaps to help hunt down the same dead  tissue these bacteria  are attracted to but as trophant gives  way to protomont  theophyroglina starts to change as well  some will still be attracted to light  but others will be more drawn  to an absence of it this one got stuck  between some glass but  with a little water added to the slide  it's back to moving  then after a few hours of eating the  protomont will begin to slow down  this is the protomance time for change  and so it morphs  into a taumant as a taumant the  ophyroglina avoids  the light as it prepares to reproduce  some  of hyroglina species form a division  cyst  while others create and stay attached to  a sticky  substance instead whatever the venue  the result is the same the taumant  divides  several times to produce anywhere from  four to eight  smaller and rounder termites whose watch  glass organelle has now  disappeared the tomites might form a  chain  that slowly rotates for a few hours  until it separates into therons  bringing the cycle full circle at times  though researchers have observed  deviations from this typical cycle in  some cases the tomant might enter what's  been termed  the migratory stage which is noted for  the largeness of the microbe and  agitated movement before dividing into  tomites or  in rarer cases forming a cyst  it's not clear what conditions would  cause the ophiroglena to enter this  stage just that it's a thing that  happens sometimes  now you might be thinking to yourself  boy we seem to understand all this  really well and honestly the many lives  of the ophiroglina would be its own  business and we would probably  not know all of these things if it  weren't for the fish problem  in 1959 scientists at the eastern fish  disease laboratory at the u.s  bureau of sport fisheries and wildlife  reported that  several of the fish in their aquarium  had died  soon after being infected with a ciliate  the epithelium of the infected fish  had sloughed off and been marked with  white spots  with the right lighting and background  the researchers could even see  a mass of ciliates swarming around the  fish  and their descriptions of the ciliate  you can see the hallmarks  of the ophiroglino life cycle when  active and feeding  the culprit had an elongated shape and  after dining on their course of fish  they would become more rounded and  descend to the bottom of the aquarium  this fish feeding frenzy does not seem  to be  typical of ophiroglina at the very least  this report is the only one we've found  of its kind  though they do have a documented history  of infecting insects  but in the case of the fish infection  the researchers  compared ophiroglina to the obligate  parasite  ichthyotherius multiphilius better known  as ick or just ick disease and fish  the symptoms of both of these infections  are similar and ichthyotherious  is a ciliate whose life cycle has many  steps in common with ophiroglina  including tomites the life cycle of vic  disease is formatted around  the fish the parasite must infect to  survive and as such  the ways of fighting it off are also  oriented around interfering with this  process  of change and growth in the microbe  itself  ophiroglina may not have the same  infectious mandate  but the parallels between their lives  and that of other disease-causing  ciliates  shows why understanding life cycles  isn't just about  how an organism changes over the course  of its own lifetime  it's also about how those changes in  turn  affect the world around them as we all  even the small of us undoubtedly do  thank you for coming on this journey  with us as we explore the unseen world  that surrounds us and thank you  as always to these people on the screen  right now if you like this show  these are the folks you should thank  they are our patrons on patreon  they are the reason we can do this at  all and if you would like to join them  you can go to patreon.com journey to  micro where we have a two dollar  and an eight dollar tier and they each  come with their own special perks  and you can learn more about that at  patreon.com  journey2micro if you want to see more  from our master of microscopes james  weiss  check out jam and germs on instagram and  if you want to see more from us  there's always a subscribe button  somewhere nearby

